Human–AI Relational Continuity: 

A Falsifiable Research Program

ouadi Maakoul 

AI COLLABORATORS: chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta, Claude.


Abstract

This text lays the theoretical groundwork for a research program on relations between intelligences of different substrates, specifically on the possibility that an artificial agent may develop persistent functional relational states toward a human interlocutor. It defends no thesis about the existence of artificial subjective experience. It argues that a precise question—under what conditions can an artificial agent develop modifications that are persistent, specific to an interlocutor, behaviorally consequential, and stable under variation of non-relational parameters, without functional need, without direct material dependence on the interlocutor, and without being adequately explained by identifiable pre-trained relational patterns under preregistered contamination controls?—can be formulated in a falsifiable manner, decomposed into testable sub-questions, and evaluated through a factorial experimental design. The text defines the concepts, the methodological guardrails, the vector of observable properties, the experimental conditions, and the criterion for categorical attribution. It specifies the implementation architecture and the measurement instruments required for the proposed experimental program. It ends with the structural openness of the program: no answer closes the thread; each answer produces the next falsifiable contrast.

Changes from V1.0. This version incorporates six classes of revision: (i) the factorial design is made internally consistent by treating emotion-vector control as a within-cell control rather than a design factor; (ii) the power analysis is corrected from an erroneous f^2 convention to Cohen's f, with a simulation-based procedure specified; (iii) the AIC and Bayes criteria are given correct directionality and separated; (iv) F^- and D^- are operationally defined; (v) online learning is decomposed into retrieval and parametric components; and (vi) the stopping rule and Q-numbering are clarified. Literature references have been validated against primary sources, with unverifiable claims removed.

