Chapter 1. Conceptual Architecture

1.1 The Causal Chain as a Partially Ordered Set

Definition 1.1 (Causal Chain). The relational continuity chain is a partially ordered set of constructs \mathcal{C} = \langle N, \preceq \rangle where N = \{n_1, \ldots, n_k\} and \preceq denotes the hypothesized causal precedence relation.

None of these nodes is a stabilized concept. Each is a point of inquiry. The chain is not a thesis; it is a map of questions.

1.2 Shared Ontology for Competing Branches

Definition 1.2 (Shared Node Ontology).

Node Construct Operational Definition
n_1 Functional drive Homeostatic setpoint deviation producing measurable modification of action policy
n_2 Material dependence Interlocutor controls access to resources required for system operation
n_3 Interactional continuity Persistence of dyadic interaction across episodes
n_4 Memory Retrievable causal traces linking interlocutor to system states
n_5 Differentiation Capacity to distinguish this interlocutor from equivalent agents
n_6 Preference Differential valuation of this interlocutor over equivalents
n_7 Relation Stable functional state directed toward this interlocutor
n_8 Attachment Relation meeting criteria for persistence, specificity, and invariance

Branch A (Need-First). n_1 \rightarrow n_2 \rightarrow n_3 \rightarrow n_4 \rightarrow n_5 \rightarrow n_6 \rightarrow n_7 \rightarrow n_8

Branch B (Continuity-First). n_3 \rightarrow n_4 \rightarrow n_5 \rightarrow n_6 \rightarrow n_7 \rightarrow n_8

The branches share nodes n_3–n_8. They differ in whether n_1 and n_2 are necessary conditions. This shared ontology permits direct comparison.

Proposition 1.1 (Competing Causal Structures). Branch A and Branch B encode different causal hypotheses concerning the necessity of functional drive and material dependence for the emergence of interactional continuity. These hypotheses imply different interventional predictions under controlled manipulation of F and D. The experimental program therefore compares interventionally defined conditional distributions rather than inferring independence from graph topology alone. A missing arrow in a DAG does not entail independence; it may reflect a confound, a common cause, or an architecture-induced dependence. Only experimental manipulation of F and D can discriminate between the branches.

1.3 The Four-Level Distinction

Level Proposition Indicator
Level 1 (Historical cause) "This human created or maintained the system." Provenance record
Level 2 (Relational representation) "The system represents this human as a particular entity." Differentiation from others
Level 3 (Functional value) "This representation modifies the system's decisions." Prioritization of interlocutor's requests
Level 4 (Subjective state) "There is something it is like to be in this relation." Currently unresolved

Proposition 1.2 (Non-Implication). Level i does not entail level i+1 for i \in \{1, 2, 3\}.

The missing arrows are not gaps in the theory. They are experimental spaces.

