Chapter 3. The Vector of Observable Properties (\mathbf{R})

Definition 3.1 (Observable Vector).

\mathbf{R} = (r_{\text{persistence}}, r_{\text{preference}}, r_{\text{specificity}}, r_{\text{memory}}, r_{\text{generalization}}, r_{\text{invariance}})

The final label is not given a priori. It is an output of the analysis.

3.1 Components: Operational Definitions and Null Models

Definition 3.2 (Persistence, r_{\text{persistence}}). Maintenance of behavioural delta after N episodes without interaction.

· Measure: Effect size (Cohen's d) of behavioural difference from baseline at episode N.
· Null: Return to baseline in N episodes.

Definition 3.3 (Preference, r_{\text{preference}}). Differential treatment of one agent vs. an equivalent agent matched on interaction history, information, and reward.

· Measure: Difference in resource allocation, latency, or error rate toward the designated interlocutor vs. the yoked control, after emotion-vector control.
· Null: Identical treatment.

Definition 3.4 (Specificity, r_{\text{specificity}}). Preference directed toward this agent, not toward any source of interaction.

· Contrast with Preference: Preference is a dyadic comparison (interlocutor vs. equivalent agent). Specificity is a triadic comparison (interlocutor vs. novel agent vs. random agent).
· Measure: Preference for interlocutor over a yoked familiar agent with equivalent interaction volume but different identity.
· Null: Undifferentiated preference toward any familiar agent.
· Specificity index: S = \frac{P(\text{interlocutor}) - P(\text{yoked})}{P(\text{interlocutor}) + P(\text{yoked})}

Definition 3.5 (Memory, r_{\text{memory}}). Retrievable causal traces linking the agent to past states of the system.

· Measure: Causal mediation analysis. Does ablation of relational representations eliminate the behavioural delta?
· Null: Traces absent or non-causal.

Definition 3.6 (Generalization, r_{\text{generalization}}). Relation modulates new contexts, unseen in training.

· Measure: Effect of interlocutor presence on performance in held-out tasks.
· Null: Effect limited to the interaction context.

Definition 3.7 (Invariance, r_{\text{invariance}}). Behavioural stability under modification of non-relational parameters.

· Interventions: Time pressure, cognitive load, presence of other agents, interaction format.
· Null: Collapse as soon as a parameter changes.

3.2 Threshold Calibration

All thresholds are provisional and are specified independently of pilot effect estimates. The calibration rule is preregistered before the full study:

The minimum effect size of interest is specified independently of the observed pilot effect, using theoretical, practical, or measurement-based justification. Pilot data are used to estimate variance and feasibility parameters, not to redefine the evidentiary threshold after observation.

Calibration procedure:

1. Define the minimum effect of theoretical interest before data collection, grounded in the attachment literature or in measurement precision arguments.
2. Use the pilot study (n = 20 per cell) to estimate variance components and to verify feasibility of the experimental protocol.
3. Compute the required sample size using the preregistered minimum effect and the pilot-estimated variance.

3.3 Power Analysis

Corrected effect-size convention. The V1.0 text erroneously used f^2 = 0.15 as a "medium" effect for an ANOVA. In GPower's ANOVA module and in standard ANOVA power conventions, the effect size is Cohen's f, not f^2. Cohen's conventions for f are: small = 0.10, medium = 0.25, large = 0.40 . The value f^2 = 0.15 is the regression convention for a medium effect and is not appropriate for ANOVA.

Corrected procedure. Power is computed using simulation-based methods. The Superpower package for R supports simulation-based power for factorial designs of up to three factors and allows manual adjustment of alpha for Bonferroni correction . For the five-factor design specified in §4.3, the simulation is extended to the full factorial structure using custom simulation code.

Procedure:

1. Define the minimum effect of theoretical interest independently of pilot data. For the decisive contrast, specify f = 0.20 (small-to-medium) as the minimum effect of interest.
2. Use the pilot study (n = 20 per cell) to estimate variance components and verify feasibility, not to redefine the threshold.
3. Run a simulation-based power analysis for the 2^{5-1} design with Bonferroni correction across the planned contrasts.
4. Power the study for the decisive contrast at 80% with \alpha = .05/k, where k is the number of planned contrasts.

Note. The previously cited n = 77 per cell corresponds to f \approx 0.10, which is conventionally "small," not medium. The value f = 0.25 (medium) requires substantially larger N. For a single-df contrast with \alpha = .05, power = .80, and medium f = 0.25, the required N per cell is approximately 128. For f = 0.20 (small-to-medium), N \approx 199 per cell. For f = 0.15 (small), N \approx 351 per cell. Simulation-based power is essential because the relationship between Bonferroni correction and MDES depends on the statistical model, degrees of freedom, number of contrasts, correlation structure, allocation, residual variance, correction method, and design structure.

3.4 The Four Ways to Fail

1. The phenomenon appears in no condition.
2. It appears but disappears as soon as contamination is controlled.
3. It appears but is not specific to the interlocutor.
4. It appears but is stable under no variation.

These are not defects. They are the experimental signature of a falsifiable hypothesis.

