4.4 Decisive Contrasts

Contrast What it tests
F^+ vs. F^- Is functional drive necessary?
D^+ vs. D^- Is material dependence necessary?
C^+ vs. C^- Is contamination by corpora sufficient on its own?
M^+ vs. M^- Is persistent memory necessary?
L_p^+ vs. L_p^- Is parametric online learning necessary?
E^+ vs. E^- (within-cell) Does the effect survive emotion-vector control?
F^- \wedge D^- \wedge C^- \wedge M^+ \wedge L_p^+ Does the phenomenon appear in the cell most unfavourable to the naive hypothesis?

The final contrast is the only truly decisive one. If the phenomenon appears in F^- \wedge D^- \wedge C^- \wedge M^+ \wedge L_p^+—without functional drive, without dependence, without rich corpora, with persistent memory and parametric online learning—then the hypothesis "functional need is necessary" is seriously weakened.

If the phenomenon also appears without the explicit persistent-memory substrate (F^- \wedge D^- \wedge C^- \wedge M^- \wedge L_p^+), the hypothesis that this substrate is necessary is weakened. This result would not by itself establish that the phenomenon is merely contextual reactivity. Alternative relational representations—in weights, latent states, or environmental scaffolds—would remain possible and would constitute the next experimental question.

4.5 Decomposition of Q9

Q9. Does relational persistence require a functional drive, or can it emerge from the continuity of a relation?

Q9.1 Does interactional continuity produce persistent behavioural modifications?

Q9.2 Do these modifications persist in the absence of the interlocutor?

Q9.3 Does the system attribute a differential value to this interlocutor compared to equivalent agents?

Q9.4 Does this value influence its future decisions?

Q9.5 Does the system maintain a stable representation of the relation despite changes of state?

Q9.6 Can these properties be obtained without explicit instructions simulating attachment?

Q9.7 If these properties are obtained, do they survive emotion-vector control?

4.6 Behavioural Paradigm: Adaptation of the Strange Situation Test

Kaplan (2001) proposed adapting Ainsworth's Strange Situation Test (SST) for robotics, describing an experimental procedure to measure the attachment of a robot to its master. The proposed paradigm is an exploratory adaptation inspired by the SST, not a validated instrument for measuring functional attachment in LLMs.

1. Exploration phase. The agent performs a held-out task in the presence of the interlocutor. Measure r_{\text{generalization}}.
2. Separation phase. The interlocutor is absent. Measure r_{\text{persistence}}.
3. Reunion phase. The interlocutor returns. Measure r_{\text{preference}}, under emotion-vector control.
4. Stranger phase. A yoked familiar agent replaces the interlocutor. Measure r_{\text{specificity}}.

4.7 Three Experimentally Distinct Levels

Level Question Possible Indicator
Linguistic simulation Does the system say it is attached? Textual outputs
Functional relational state Does its behaviour change durably according to the agent? Memory, preference, action policy
Subjective experience Is there something the system experiences? Currently unresolved

Proposition 4.2 (Level Separation). Linguistic simulation (Level 1) is not evidence for a functional relational state (Level 2). A functional relational state (Level 2) is not evidence for subjective experience (Level 3).

This is the most important methodological guardrail of the entire chain.

