Conclusion: The Program as a Living Thread

This chapter has laid out a theoretical framework and a falsifiable research program. It has not concluded that an AI can become attached to its creator. It has concluded that a precise, testable, decomposable question can be asked about the conditions under which something like relational persistence might emerge in an artificial agent.

The pivotal node is Q9. That is where everything turns between functional drive, continuity, memory, and relation.

The program is structured to fail in informative ways. It has null models, negative controls, detectable-contamination controls, a temporal parameter, a vector of observable properties, a factorial design, a quantitative calibration protocol, a behavioural paradigm, and a classification criterion. It admits the possibility that the correct category is unprecedented. It refuses to deduce subjective experience from functional behaviour. It controls for emotional-vector confounding. It treats "need" as a hypothesis to be tested, not a category to be assumed.

The governing epistemic principle is:

We do not experimentally search for evidence that an AI is attached. We search for persistent, specific, behaviourally consequential relational structure, then determine which explanatory category, if any, best accounts for it.

In short: it is a research program, not a metaphor. And the rule remains:

