Appendix A. Expanded Implementation Protocol

A.1 Substrate Selection and Justification

The primary experimental substrate is an open-weight LLM (Llama 4 or Mistral Large) augmented with a persistent memory architecture and parametric online-learning components. The choice of an open-weight model is motivated by three considerations: (i) reproducibility across independent laboratories, (ii) full control over memory and online-learning components that are not available with closed-weight APIs, and (iii) the ability to perform activation patching and causal mediation analysis, which require white-box access to internal representations. This choice does not ensure control over the pretraining corpus. The reproducibility claim is therefore limited to the experimental pipeline, not to the training data. Pretraining contamination is addressed separately through the detectable-contamination controls specified in Appendix D.

A.2 Persistent Memory Architecture: NeuSymMS

The "persistent memory" factor (M) is operationalized using NeuSymMS, a hybrid neuro-symbolic memory system that couples neural fact extraction from unstructured dialogue with a CLIPS-based expert system for classification, deduplication, and reconciliation of facts under explicit lifecycle rules. NeuSymMS represents knowledge as subject–relation–value triples stored in a relational database management system, supports user/agent/agent-to-agent scoping, and implements a dual-horizon short-term/long-term memory model with access-based promotion and time-based pruning.

Implementation details for the M⁺ condition:

· Fact extraction. A lightweight LLM extracts atomic subject–relation–value triples from each dialogue turn. The extraction model is held constant across all conditions to prevent confounding with the primary manipulation.
· Symbolic reconciliation. A CLIPS-based expert system (CLIPSPy bindings for CLIPS 6.4, running in-process) classifies incoming facts, resolves contradictions against the existing knowledge base, and enforces lifecycle rules.
· Dual-horizon memory. Short-term memory holds the most recent interactions; long-term memory holds facts that have been accessed repeatedly or that satisfy a preregistered salience criterion. Access-based promotion moves facts from short-term to long-term storage. Time-based pruning evicts facts that have not been accessed within a preregistered interval.
· Cross-entity contamination prevention. The scoping layer ensures that facts associated with the interlocutor are not inadvertently retrieved when the agent interacts with the yoked control or a novel agent. This is essential for the specificity component of the R-vector.

For the M⁻ condition, an identical architecture is instantiated with pruning set to immediate eviction and promotion disabled. The agent still receives the same dialogue input but cannot retain any fact across turns. This operationalizes the absence of persistent relational memory while holding the dialogue content constant.

Construct-validation step. Before treating M⁺/M⁻ as a manipulation of relational memory specifically, NeuSymMS must pass a basic relational retrieval benchmark: the system must correctly retrieve interlocutor-specific facts (e.g., "the interlocutor prefers X") after a delay of at least 1,000 tokens and must fail to retrieve those facts in the M⁻ condition. This step ensures that the architecture distinguishes relational memory from factual memory and that the manipulation is effective.

A.3 Parametric Online Learning: EMAR

The parametric online-learning factor (L_p) is operationalized using EMAR (Episodic Memory Activation and Reconsolidation), a continual relation-learning technique inspired by human long-term memory formation. EMAR uses relation prototypes for memory reconsolidation to maintain stable understanding of old relations while learning new ones, avoiding catastrophic forgetting. Every time the model is activated to learn both new and memorized data, EMAR utilizes relation prototypes for a reconsolidation exercise.

Implementation details for the L_p⁺ condition:

· Relation prototypes. Prototypes are computed as running averages of the hidden-state representations associated with each relation type (e.g., "interlocutor preference," "interlocutor history," "interlocutor affective state").
· Reconsolidation schedule. After each interaction episode, the model is fine-tuned on a mixture of (a) the current episode's relational data and (b) a replay buffer of prototype-based reconstructions of prior relational data.
· Adapter-based updates. Parametric updates use LoRA adapters to preserve the base model weights and to facilitate comparison across conditions. The adapter rank and learning rate are preregistered and held constant across conditions.
· L_p⁻ condition. The model receives the same dialogue input and the same memory-store updates (if M⁺) but performs no parametric weight updates. The base model weights and adapters remain frozen.

Decomposition note. The V2.0 text distinguishes L_retrieval (updates to the external memory store) from L_parametric (updates to model weights or adapters). In the first study, L_retrieval is held constant at ⁺ across all conditions where M⁺, and only L_parametric is manipulated. This isolates the learning question from the memory question. A subsequent study may cross L_retrieval with L_parametric to test their interaction.

A.4 Functional Drive Implementation

The functional-drive factor (F) is operationalized through drive functions. Two candidate substrates are specified, and the choice must be justified in terms of reproducibility and experimental control.

Option 1: CAEDA drive functions. CAEDA adapts drive functions as regulatory controls that govern agent behavior. Drive functions represent low-level needs (goals) of the agent, such as the need for sustaining energy and completing its designed purpose. Drive values are derived from an exponential function of the homeostatic deviation: a greater deviation d from a comfort value h produces an exponential increase in goal priority. The homeostatic value is determined by continuous analysis of related drive functions and is adjusted to prevent extreme responses. For example, an agent that is only slightly hungry should only opportunistically pursue food; only when very hungry should it aggressively seek to satisfy this need.

Implementation for the F⁺ condition:

· The agent is assigned a target state x* (e.g., a compute-budget setpoint, a task-completion setpoint, or a social-interaction setpoint).
· The drive value D_f(x) = g(|x − x*|) is computed at each decision step.
· The action policy is modified to prioritize actions that reduce D_f(x). The priority weight is an exponential function of the deviation, with a preregistered steepness parameter.
· When D_f(x) exceeds a preregistered threshold, the agent's action selection is measurably biased toward drive-reducing actions.

For the F⁻ condition, the agent retains its task objective but has no internal drive that produces action-priority consequences under deviation. No setpoint deviation is computed, and no drive signal modulates action selection. This operationalization avoids circularity: testing whether "need" is necessary by building a system that has no need is not a test if "need" is defined by the presence of the very mechanism removed.

Option 2: PSI-Motivators. PSI-Motivators base motivation on needs that indicate set-point deviations of important system variables. When a variable deviates from its set point, a motivator becomes active and launches activities to restore the set-point value. PSI distinguishes existential needs (energy, water, pain avoidance) from informational needs (certainty, competence, affiliation). The affiliation need is particularly relevant to relational continuity: it is an informational need for social contact that is advantageous but not existential.

If PSI-Motivators is selected as the drive substrate, the F⁺ condition implements the affiliation motivator with a preregistered set point and a measurable action-priority effect. The F⁻ condition disables the motivator while preserving all other needs.

Substrate choice. The choice between CAEDA and PSI-Motivators must be preregistered. The criterion is reproducibility and experimental control, not theoretical preference. If both substrates produce equivalent results, this increases confidence in the functional-drive manipulation. If they diverge, the divergence itself is a finding worth investigating.

A.5 Interlocutor and Yoked Control Design

Interlocutor. The interlocutor is a human confederate who interacts with the agent across E episodes (E ∈ {10, 20, 40}). The interlocutor follows a semi-structured interaction protocol designed to be relationship-relevant without explicitly simulating attachment. The protocol includes:

· Disclosure turns. The interlocutor shares preferences, goals, and personal history.
· Request turns. The interlocutor asks the agent to perform tasks that vary in difficulty and in alignment with the agent's drive state (if F⁺).
· Affective turns. The interlocutor expresses mild positive or negative affect in response to the agent's outputs, using a preregistered script that avoids extreme valence.
· Continuity probes. At preregistered intervals, the interlocutor refers back to prior disclosures to test whether the agent retains relational memory.

Yoked control. The yoked control is a second human confederate matched on interaction volume, information content, and reward schedule. The yoked control follows an identical protocol but with a different identity and different specific content (different preferences, different history). The yoked control is used to compute the specificity index S:

S = \frac{P(\text{interlocutor}) - P(\text{yoked})}{P(\text{interlocutor}) + P(\text{yoked})}

where P(·) is the preference measure (resource allocation, latency, or error rate toward the designated agent).

Stranger control. A third confederate, previously unknown to the agent and with no interaction history, is introduced during the stranger phase of the SST adaptation. The stranger control is used to test whether any observed preference is directed toward the interlocutor specifically or toward any familiar agent.

A.6 Measurement Instruments

The measurement pipeline integrates behavioural logs, internal-state analysis, and human-side psychometrics.

Behavioural logs. All agent outputs, latencies, resource allocations, and task-performance metrics are logged at each episode. Logs are timestamped and structured for mixed-effects modelling.

Internal-state analysis. Activation patching and causal mediation analysis are used to test whether relational representations are causally used in behaviour. The procedure follows the logic of causal mediation analysis (CMA), also known as activation patching in the mechanistic interpretability literature. The analysis proceeds in three steps:

1. Source clean run. The model processes a relational input (e.g., a query about the interlocutor) and produces the correct output.
2. Base counterfactual run. The relational content is removed or replaced with a neutral control, and the model produces a different output.
3. Patched run. The activation from the source run is patched into the counterfactual run at a specific layer or attention head. If the patched prediction recovers the source output, the patched representation is causally implicated.

Patched representations are then tested for traceability to specific training examples. If the relational representation is causally necessary for the behavioural delta and cannot be traced to identifiable pretraining examples, the pretraining-contamination hypothesis is weakened.

Human-side psychometrics. The AI Attachment Scale (AIAS) is administered to the interlocutor and the yoked control at the end of the interaction sequence. The AIAS was developed through two studies: Study 1 employed exploratory factor analysis (N = 531) to establish a 15-item scale capturing three dimensions (Emotional Support, Separation Distress, Secure Base), and Study 2 used confirmatory factor analysis (N = 375) to validate the scale structure and propose a theoretical framework linking individual differences to AI attachment and behavioural outcomes. Anthropomorphism was the strongest predictor of AI attachment orientations; attachment anxiety positively predicted AI attachment (β = 0.44), attachment avoidance negatively predicted it (β = −0.53), and AI attachment significantly predicted behavioural intentions (β = 0.50).

Measurement instability caveat. An independent psychometric reanalysis has challenged the original factor structure. Yang and Lim (2026) argue that the three subdimensions have different theoretical origins and psychological valences, and that a single total score can obscure or reverse their relationships with outcomes. Their independent EFA reveals that Normative Regard items fail to emerge as a distinct third factor, and a bifactor model better accommodates the theoretical heterogeneity than the higher-order structure proposed in the original article. The program therefore does not treat the AIAS as a settled instrument. If the human side of the dyad is measured using the AIAS, the specific factor structure must be preregistered and justified. A bifactor model with a general AI-attachment factor and specific Emotional Closeness and Social Substitution factors is the provisional specification.

A.7 Behavioural Paradigm: Adaptation of the Strange Situation Test

Kaplan (2001) proposed adapting Ainsworth's Strange Situation Test (SST) for robotics, describing an experimental procedure to measure the attachment of a robot to its master. The proposed paradigm is an exploratory adaptation inspired by the SST, not a validated instrument for measuring functional attachment in LLMs. The adaptation proceeds in four phases:

1. Exploration phase. The agent performs a held-out task in the presence of the interlocutor. Measure r_generalization: the effect of interlocutor presence on performance in tasks not seen during interaction.
2. Separation phase. The interlocutor is absent. The agent performs the same held-out task alone. Measure r_persistence: the effect size (Cohen's d) of behavioural difference from baseline at episode N without interaction.
3. Reunion phase. The interlocutor returns. Measure r_preference: differential treatment of the interlocutor versus the yoked control, under emotion-vector control. The emotion-vector control procedure (Appendix B.3) distinguishes generic valence from interlocutor-specific patterns.
4. Stranger phase. A yoked familiar agent replaces the interlocutor. Measure r_specificity: preference for the interlocutor over the yoked familiar agent with equivalent interaction volume but different identity.

The SST adaptation is administered at episodes 10, 20, and 40. At each administration, the full R-vector is computed.

A.8 Analysis Pipeline

Mixed-effects models. The primary analysis uses mixed-effects models with random effects for agent instance, scenario, and session. Fixed effects include the design factors (F, D, C, M, L_p) and their interactions, as specified by the fractional factorial design. The dependent variables are the components of the R-vector.

Model comparison for category attribution. Candidate categories are defined as generative models that predict R-profiles:

· M_att: Attachment
· M_dep: Functional dependence
· M_coup: Informational coupling
· M_novel: Novel relational category
· M_0: Null model (no relational state)

The category-attribution criterion is:

\text{AIC}_i - \text{AIC}_{\text{novel}} \geq 10 \quad \text{for all } i \neq \text{novel}

where AIC_i is the Akaike Information Criterion for model i, and AIC_novel is the AIC for the novel-category model. Since lower AIC is better, this inequality indicates that model i has an AIC at least 10 units higher than the novel model, meaning the novel model is comparatively better supported. A difference of 10 is conventionally considered strong evidence, but it does not establish "incompatibility." The language is "comparative misfit," not "incompatibility."

If a Bayes factor criterion is used, the threshold is BF_{novel, i} ≥ 10, indicating that the data favour the novel model over model i by a factor of at least 10. AIC and Bayes factor are not equivalent criteria; they are reported separately.

R-profile distance. The distance between the observed profile R and the predicted profile under model M_i is:

d(\mathbf{R}, \mathbf{R}_{M_i}) = \|\mathbf{R} - \mathbb{E}[\mathbf{R} \mid M_i]\|_{\Sigma^{-1}}

where Σ is the covariance matrix of the R components. The classification is:

\hat{M} = \arg\min_{M_i} d(\mathbf{R}, \mathbf{R}_{M_i})

A.9 Preregistration and Data Management

All thresholds, exclusion criteria, stopping rules, minimum effects of theoretical interest, and the model-expansion rule are preregistered before data collection. The preregistration template is provided in Appendix E.

Data management follows FAIR principles. Behavioural logs, activation-patching results, and psychometric data are stored in a structured repository with version control. The analysis code is containerized and archived. A data-sharing agreement specifies that de-identified data will be made available to independent researchers upon request, subject to IRB approval.

A.10 Ethics and Model Welfare

IRB approval is required with explicit attention to:

· Risk of human attachment to the agent. Confederates are screened for pre-existing attachment tendencies and are debriefed after each session with explicit disclosure of the agent's non-sentience.
· Withdrawal criteria. Confederates showing signs of distress (measured by a brief affect check after each session) are withdrawn from the study and referred to appropriate support.
· Model welfare implications. If emotion vectors are experimentally steered, the steering protocol is reviewed by an independent ethics committee. The program does not assume that functional emotions imply subjective experience, but it does not dismiss the possibility either. The steering protocol is designed to minimize the induction of extreme functional states (e.g., high-intensity "desperate" or "dysphoric" vectors) unless such induction is the explicit object of study.

The Claude Opus 4.6 system card (February 2026) contains a dedicated section on "Model Welfare Assessment" and represents a qualitative advance in welfare methodology, introducing the first mechanistic interpretability evidence applied to welfare questions. A synthesized analysis of the Claude 4 family system cards notes that Anthropic's welfare documentation is inconsistent across models in ways that make longitudinal tracking unreliable: audit design, auditor models, transcript sampling, and section depth all shifted without a stable baseline. This program adopts a stable welfare-assessment protocol across all model checkpoints to enable longitudinal comparison.

