Appendix B. Measurement Instruments and Operationalizations

B.1 AI Attachment Scale (Human Side)

The AIAS is a 15-item self-report instrument with three subscales: Emotional Closeness, Social Substitution, and Normative Regard. Items are rated on a 7-point Likert scale. The scale was developed and validated in two studies: Study 1 (N = 531, exploratory factor analysis) established the three-factor structure, and Study 2 (N = 375, confirmatory factor analysis) validated the structure and tested a theoretical framework linking individual differences to AI attachment and behavioural outcomes.

Provisional scoring. If the bifactor model is supported by the preregistered confirmatory analysis, scores are computed for a general AI-attachment factor and for the specific Emotional Closeness and Social Substitution factors. The Normative Regard subscale is retained as a separate observed variable but is not treated as a distinct latent factor unless the bifactor model shows adequate fit.

Predictors. Anthropomorphism is measured using the Individual Differences in Anthropomorphism Questionnaire (IDAQ). Adult attachment orientation is measured using the Experiences in Close Relationships–Revised (ECR-R) scale.

