Appendix C. Statistical Analysis Plan

C.1 Power Analysis (Corrected)

The V1.0 text erroneously used f² = 0.15 as a "medium" effect for an ANOVA. In GPower's ANOVA module and in standard ANOVA power conventions, the effect size is Cohen's f, not f². Cohen's conventions for f are: small = 0.10, medium = 0.25, large = 0.40. The value f² = 0.15 is the regression convention for a medium effect and is not appropriate for ANOVA.

Corrected procedure. Power is computed using simulation-based methods. The Superpower package for R supports simulation-based power for factorial designs of up to three factors and allows manual adjustment of alpha for Bonferroni correction. For the five-factor design specified in §4.3, the simulation is extended to the full factorial structure using custom simulation code.

Procedure:

1. Define the minimum effect of theoretical interest independently of pilot data. For the decisive contrast, specify f = 0.20 (small-to-medium) as the minimum effect of interest.
2. Use the pilot study (n = 20 per cell) to estimate variance components and verify feasibility, not to redefine the threshold.
3. Run a simulation-based power analysis for the 2^{5−1} design with Bonferroni correction across the planned contrasts.
4. Power the study for the decisive contrast at 80% with α = .05/k, where k is the number of planned contrasts.

Sample-size implications. For a single-df contrast with α = .05, power = .80, and medium f = 0.25, the required N per cell is approximately 128. For f = 0.20 (small-to-medium), N ≈ 199 per cell. For f = 0.15 (small), N ≈ 351 per cell. Simulation-based power is essential because the relationship between Bonferroni correction and MDES depends on the statistical model, degrees of freedom, number of contrasts, correlation structure, allocation, residual variance, correction method, and design structure.

