A new category is justified if:

\text{AIC}_i - \text{AIC}_{\text{novel}} \geq 10 \quad \text{for all } i \neq \text{novel}

and if this comparative advantage is stable across conditions. The V1.0 text wrote ΔAIC(M_novel, M_i) ≥ 10, which is ambiguous. Since lower AIC is better, the correct statement is AIC_i − AIC_novel ≥ 10.

Bayesian criterion (separate). If a Bayes factor criterion is used, the threshold is BF_{novel, i} ≥ 10, indicating that the data favour the novel model over model i by a factor of at least 10. AIC and Bayes factor are not equivalent criteria; they are reported separately.

Discovery procedure for novel categories.

1. Phase 1: Pre-register candidate categories.
2. Phase 2: Collect training data.
3. Phase 3: Fit existing models.
4. Phase 4: Generate the novel-category model only under a preregistered model-expansion rule.
5. Phase 5: Evaluate the expanded model on held-out data.
