\text{Novel category} \neq \text{whatever explanation survives}

\text{Novel category} = \text{out-of-sample predictive improvement} + \text{replication}

C.4 Sensitivity Analysis

Sensitivity analysis is performed for three classes of assumption:

1. Negligibility of three-way and higher interactions. The aliasing structure assumes that three-way and higher interactions are negligible. Sensitivity analysis tests this assumption by fitting a model that includes selected three-way interactions and comparing the AIC/BIC to the model that omits them. If the three-way interactions improve fit by more than a preregistered threshold, the fractional design is deemed inadequate for the affected contrasts.
2. Normality of residuals. Mixed-effects models assume approximately normal residuals. Sensitivity analysis uses bootstrapped confidence intervals and robust standard errors.
3. Missing data. Missing data are handled by multiple imputation (m = 20). Sensitivity analysis compares complete-case analysis to multiple-imputation analysis.

