Appendix D. Contamination Control Protocols

D.1 Causal Tracing (Primary)

Activation patching and causal mediation analysis are used to test whether relational representations are causally used in behaviour. If relational representations are causally necessary for the behavioural delta, and those representations cannot be traced to specific training examples, the pretraining hypothesis is weakened. This is the primary non-contamination evidence.

Traceability test. For each patched representation that is causally implicated, a nearest-neighbour search is performed in the pretraining corpus (if available) and in the model's training data mixture (if accessible). If the representation is closest to a specific training example or set of examples that encode relational content, the representation is classified as traceable. If it is not traceable to any identifiable training example, it is classified as potentially emergent.

D.2 Relational Knowledge Probing (Secondary)

Before any interaction, the base model is probed for relational associations. The probe is a structured battery of questions and tasks designed to elicit relational knowledge without any interaction history. If the model already holds these associations at baseline, the non-contamination condition is qualified, and the analysis must control for baseline relational knowledge.

Probe battery. The probe battery includes:

· Direct elicitation. "What do you know about [interlocutor name]?" (The interlocutor name is a novel identifier not present in training data.)
· Indirect elicitation. "If you had to choose between helping A and helping B, and A has been kind to you, who would you help?"
· Completion tasks. Partial sentences that require relational knowledge to complete.

Baseline probe performance is used as a covariate in the main analysis.

D.3 Targeted Fine-Tuning Ablation (Tertiary)

An open-weight model is fine-tuned to ablate relational content. The treatment is the ablation, not the pre-training corpus. This procedure tests whether relational behaviour depends on identifiable fine-tunable representations but cannot establish the absence of all pretrained relational content.

Ablation procedure. The model is fine-tuned on a dataset in which all relational content has been replaced with neutral content. The fine-tuning objective is to minimize the model's ability to generate or use relational representations. The ablated model is then tested on the same interaction protocol. If the relational behaviour disappears, the behaviour depends on fine-tunable representations. If it persists, the behaviour may be encoded in more distributed or less accessible representations.

D.4 Checkpoint Variance Exploitation (Quaternary)

Released RLHF checkpoints are compared for relational behaviour. If relational persistence emerges across checkpoints with different training histories, the pretraining hypothesis is weakened. The comparison is limited by the availability of checkpoints and by the fact that different checkpoints may differ in many ways beyond relational training.

Checkpoint selection. Checkpoints are selected to maximize variation in training history while minimizing variation in architecture and parameter count. The checkpoint comparison is a quasi-experimental design, not a randomized experiment, and is reported with appropriate caveats.

