Appendix E. Preregistration Template

E.1 Hypotheses and Falsification Conditions

Hypothesis Falsification Condition
H1: Functional drive is necessary for relational persistence. Relational persistence appears in F⁻ conditions with effect size ≥ minimum effect of interest, after Bonferroni correction.
H2: Material dependence is necessary for relational persistence. Relational persistence appears in D⁻ conditions with effect size ≥ minimum effect of interest.
H3: Rich relational pretraining corpora are sufficient for relational persistence. Relational persistence appears in C⁻ conditions with effect size ≥ minimum effect of interest.
H4: Persistent memory is necessary for relational persistence. Relational persistence appears in M⁻ conditions with effect size ≥ minimum effect of interest.
H5: Parametric online learning is necessary for relational persistence. Relational persistence appears in L_p⁻ conditions with effect size ≥ minimum effect of interest.
H6: The phenomenon is explainable by emotion-vector activation. The effect survives emotion-vector control (E⁺) in the decisive cell.

E.2 Design and Sample Size

· Design. 2^{5−1} fractional factorial, resolution V.
· Sample size. Determined by simulation-based power analysis for the decisive contrast (F⁻ ∧ D⁻ ∧ C⁻ ∧ M⁺ ∧ L_p⁺) at 80% power with Bonferroni correction.
· Minimum effect of interest. f = 0.20 (small-to-medium), specified independently of pilot data.
· Pilot. n = 20 per cell, used for variance estimation and feasibility, not for threshold redefinition.

E.3 Exclusion Criteria

· Agent exclusion. Runs with technical failures (memory corruption, adapter divergence, API errors) are excluded. The exclusion rate is preregistered at < 5%. If the exclusion rate exceeds 5%, the run is repeated.
· Confederate exclusion. Confederates scoring above a preregistered threshold on the ECR-R anxiety subscale or showing signs of distress are excluded.
· Data exclusion. Sessions with incomplete logs or missing R-vector components are excluded. The exclusion rule is applied blind to condition.

E.4 Stopping Rules

A sub-question is resolved (not "closed") when:

1. the falsification condition has been tested under at least two architectures;
2. the effect survives Bonferroni correction;
3. the result has been replicated in at least one independent sample.

A resolved sub-question produces a new question. The thread remains open.

E.5 Model Expansion Rule

A novel-category model is added to the candidate set only if:

1. the existing models (M_att, M_dep, M_coup, M_0) all show comparative misfit (AIC_i − AIC_novel ≥ 10 or BF_{novel,i} ≥ 10);
2. the novel model is specified before examining held-out data;
3. the novel model produces out-of-sample predictive improvement;
4. the improvement is replicated on an independent architecture.

